{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Who is YHWH that I should obey His voice.</b> They said in the Midrash: at that hour Pharaoh brought out the register of deity, and began calling: the god of Edom, the god of Moab, the god of Sidon. He said to them: 'Behold I have called, and there is no name written in my register for the name of your Elohim.' And the explanation of the matter is that Pharaoh, in his great wisdom, knew the boundaries of the lands and the limits of habitation, and that the entire world is divided into seven climates, and he knew the powers of the stars that rule in each and every climate, and over which nation of the nations they rule — likewise in the seven climates and likewise in the seventy nations. And since he found for this name no dominion or rule anywhere on earth among the rest of the powers known to him, he was astonished and said: 'Who is YHWH that I should obey His voice,' for there is no power ruling on earth that is not written in my register; and he did not know that this name is supreme over them all, and that all the rest of the powers ruling on earth have no dominion or strength except from His strength. And our Rabbis of blessed memory offered a parable for this — to what may the matter be compared? To a priest who had a foolish servant. The priest went outside the city; the servant went outside the city to seek his master. He arrived at a cemetery and began crying out to the people standing there: 'Have you seen my master here?' They said to him: 'Is your master not a priest?' He said to them: 'Yes.' They said to him: 'Fool! Who has ever seen a priest in a cemetery? What business has a king in a slaughterhouse, what business has a priest in a cemetery?' So said Moses and Aaron: 'These deities that you speak of are dead, but our Elohim is a living Elohim and King of the world.' He said to them: 'And what are His deeds?' They said to him: 'He stretches out the heavens and founds the earth, shatters mountains and breaks rocks, brings down rain and causes grass to sprout, deposes kings and raises up kings.' He said to them: 'I am the lord of the world, and I created myself and the river Nile, as it is said (Ezekiel 29:3): ״לי יאורי ואני עשיתני״ — \"My Nile is mine, and I have made myself.\" Who is YHWH that I should obey His voice?' The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'Wicked one! You shall be stricken through water!' — water was turned to blood for him. <br><b>Another interpretation:</b> 'Who is YHWH that I should obey His voice' — that I would be obligated to obey His voice — why, He has never come to me. He came to Avimelekh in a dream of the night and said to him (Genesis 20:6): ״בתם לבבך עשית זאת״ — \"you did this in the integrity of your heart,\" and ״וינגע ה' את פרעה״ — 'and YHWH struck Pharaoh' with great plagues; yet since this is so, he does not recognize Him — <b>I do not know YHWH, and moreover I will not send Israel.</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:23.053304909Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}